## 3. What confidence means

> **In one sentence:** confidence is the probability that the final MERC lands within an agreed tolerance of the true rebuild cost.

### Two kinds of error

Even after the quote flow, the final MERC can miss the true rebuild cost for two separate reasons. We measure both on the log scale, so they simply add:

$$
\underbrace{E}_{\text{total error}} \;=\; \underbrace{D}_{\text{leftover attribute error}} \;+\; \underbrace{\varepsilon_V}_{\text{engine error}}
$$

Here $E = \ln(\text{true rebuild cost}) - \ln(\text{final MERC})$, so a positive $E$ means the MERC is too low.

| | **Leftover attribute error** $D$ | **Engine error** $\varepsilon_V$ |
|---|---|---|
| **What it is** | An attribute we didn't ask about is wrong, so Verisk priced the wrong house | Verisk's estimate isn't exact, even for the right house |
| **Could a question fix it?** | Yes, but we chose not to ask | No |
| **What tells us about it** | Attribute confidence × how much MERC would move | The range Verisk returns |

> 💡 **Why logs?** On the log scale, a difference of 0.05 is about 5%, and effects that multiply (area × cost per square foot × adjustments) become effects that add. That's what lets us build the score one attribute at a time. Tables in this document show impacts as percentages; the math underneath runs on logs.

### The tolerance

The tolerance says how far off the MERC may be and still count as right. It should be **asymmetric**:

- A MERC that's **too low** leaves the home underinsured and leaks premium. Keep this side tight.
- A MERC that's **too high** makes the quote less competitive. This side can be looser.

$$
C \;=\; P\big(-\tau_{\text{over}} \;\le\; E \;\le\; \tau_{\text{under}}\big)
$$

↳ **In words:** $C$ is the chance that the MERC is no more than $\tau_{\text{under}}$ too low and no more than $\tau_{\text{over}}$ too high.

This document uses $\tau_{\text{under}} = 0.10$ and $\tau_{\text{over}} = 0.15$ (about 10% and 15%) for illustration. The real values are a business decision. They must be comfortably wider than Verisk's typical range, or engine error alone would keep every quote out of High.

