## 7. Ingredient 2: Engine uncertainty

> **In one sentence:** Verisk's range tells us how precise its estimate is for the right house; we turn it into a spread and fold it into the simulation.

### 7.1 First, confirm what the range means

The range might reflect cost uncertainty only (labor, materials, finish), or it might also include Verisk's own uncertainty about the attributes. That matters, because leftover attribute risk already covers the attributes, and counting them twice would understate confidence.

**A quick test on about 50 homes:** call Verisk once with the prefill as returned, and once with every attribute passed explicitly.

| What happens to the range | What it means | What to do |
|---|---|---|
| It **stays the same** | It reflects cost uncertainty only | Use it as is |
| It **narrows** when attributes are passed | It includes attribute uncertainty | In step 5, always pass every final attribute explicitly, and use that range |

While you're at it, ask Verisk what coverage the range is meant to have, such as 90%.

### 7.2 Turn the range into a spread

$$
\sigma_V \;=\; \frac{\ln U - \ln L}{2 \times 1.645}
$$

↳ **In words:** the width of the range on the log scale, divided by the width of a 90% Normal interval. $U$ and $L$ are the top and bottom of the range. Use 1.645 for a 90% range and 1.96 for a 95% range. If Verisk can't state the coverage, start at 90% and revisit.

### 7.3 Combine with leftover attribute risk

For each simulated draw $D_i$, the chance that Verisk's own noise still leaves the MERC in tolerance has a closed form. Averaging across the draws gives the confidence:

$$
C \;=\; \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left[\,\Phi\!\left(\frac{\tau_{\text{under}} - D_i}{\sigma_V}\right) - \Phi\!\left(\frac{-\tau_{\text{over}} - D_i}{\sigma_V}\right)\right]
$$

↳ **In words:** for each possible true house, take the chance that engine noise keeps the MERC in tolerance, then average over all the possible houses. $\Phi$ is the standard Normal CDF and $N$ is the number of draws.

> 📌 **In practice:** also keep the **attribute-only** version: the share of draws where $D$ alone is within tolerance. It's the part we can validate directly ([section 11](#11-proving-it-works)).

