## 9. From probability to High, Medium, Low

> **In one sentence:** $C$ sets the base tier, and the neighborhood score can only lower it.

**Step 1 · Base tier from $C$**

| Confidence $C$ | Base tier |
|:-:|:-:|
| 0.90 or more | 🟢 High |
| 0.70 up to 0.90 | 🟡 Medium |
| Below 0.70 | 🔴 Low |

**Step 2 · Neighborhood override**

| Neighborhood score | What happens |
|:-:|---|
| $\lvert z \rvert \le 2$ | Keep the base tier |
| $2 < \lvert z \rvert \le 3$ | Drop one tier |
| $\lvert z \rvert > 3$ | Force 🔴 Low |

These cutoffs are **illustrative starting points**; [section 11](#11-proving-it-works) explains how to set them from data. If we can put rough costs on MERC error (premium leakage, underinsurance exposure) and on inspections, the thresholds on $C$ can come from expected cost instead of judgment.

> 💡 **Why an override instead of one blended number?** It's easy to explain ("the neighborhood check flagged it"), and each part can be tuned and validated on its own.

### Reason codes

Every score should come with its **main driver**: the attribute whose confirmation would raise $C$ the most. To find it, rerun the calculation with each attribute treated as known:

$$
\text{Lift}_k \;=\; C_{\text{with } k \text{ known}} \;-\; C
$$

This tells underwriting *why* a MERC is Medium or Low, and which single fact would fix it. That's exactly what's needed to route a quote to inspection.

