## 10. Worked example

> **In one sentence:** a 1-story, 2,150 sq ft home lands at 🟡 Medium, and the reason is almost entirely square footage.

All numbers are illustrative. Every figure below was computed with the code in [Appendix C](#appendix-c-reference-code).

### The quote

| Quote detail | Value |
|---|---|
| **Final MERC** | \$372,000 |
| **Verisk range (90%)** | \$341,000 to \$405,000 |
| **Square footage** | 2,150 sq ft, so \$173 per sq ft |
| **Member confirmed** | Quality grade ("standard") and garage (corrected to 2-car attached) |
| **Not asked** | Square footage, stories, construction, roof, year built |
| **Tolerance** | MERC no more than 10% too low, and no more than 15% too high |

### Step 1 · Leftover attribute risk

| Not-asked attribute | $p_k$ | If wrong, it's usually… | MERC impact if wrong | Materiality $m_k$ |
|---|:-:|---|:-:|:-:|
| Square footage | 0.86 | 18% larger (55%) or 12% smaller (45%) | +15% or −10% | **0.018** |
| Stories | 0.93 | 2 stories, not 1 | −6% | 0.004 |
| Construction | 0.95 | Masonry veneer, not frame | +5% | 0.003 |
| Roof | 0.96 | Metal, not shingle | +2% | 0.001 |
| Year built | 0.94 | 10 years older or newer | ±1% | 0.001 |

The square-footage impacts use an elasticity of 0.85 from the sensitivity table.

**What the simulation shows:**

- In **69%** of draws, every not-asked attribute is right, and the leftover error is zero.
- In **7%** of draws, the MERC is more than 10% too low, because the home is really larger.
- In **0.4%** of draws, the MERC is more than 15% too high.
- Attribute-only confidence: **0.92**.

### Step 2 · Engine uncertainty

$$
\sigma_V \;=\; \frac{\ln 405{,}000 - \ln 341{,}000}{2 \times 1.645} \;=\; 0.052
$$

That's about ±5% at one standard deviation. Even if every attribute were certain, this home's confidence would top out at **0.97**.

### Step 3 · Confidence and base tier

Combining the two gives **$C$ = 0.893 → 🟡 Medium**, just short of the 0.90 needed for High.

### Step 4 · Neighborhood check

| H3 level | Homes in cell | Cell median \$/sqft | Weight $w$ | Blended center |
|:-:|--:|--:|:-:|--:|
| State | 48,000 | \$168 | — | \$168.0 |
| r4 | 2,400 | \$176 | 0.99 | \$175.9 |
| r5 | 310 | \$179 | 0.93 | \$178.8 |
| r6 | 42 | \$184 | 0.63 | \$182.0 |
| r7 | 9 | \$190 | 0.26 | \$184.1 |

This uses $k = 25$ homes at every level. The r7 cell's own median of \$190 gets only 26% of the weight: with nine homes, it mostly borrows from r6. The blended spread works out to 0.099, or about 10%.

$$
z \;=\; \frac{\ln 173 - \ln 184.1}{0.099} \;=\; -0.63
$$

That's well within ±2, so the tier stays 🟡 **Medium**.

### Step 5 · Main driver

| If we had also confirmed… | New $C$ | Lift |
|---|:-:|:-:|
| **Square footage** | **0.961** 🟢 | **+0.068** |
| Construction | 0.899 | +0.006 |
| Roof, year built or stories | 0.893 to 0.894 | under 0.002 |

> 💡 **The takeaway:** square footage cleared the attribute threshold at 86%, but it's also the attribute MERC is most sensitive to. Surfacing that combination is exactly what this score is for. Over time, reason codes like this one show which attributes most often hold MERC confidence back. That's useful evidence if the step 4 thresholds are ever revisited.

### What the output looks like

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **MERC** | \$372,000 (Verisk range \$341,000 to \$405,000) |
| **Confidence** | 0.893 → 🟡 **Medium** |
| **Neighborhood check** | \$173/sqft vs. \$184 nearby (z = −0.6), no flag |
| **Main driver** | Square footage: not asked, 86% confident. Confirming it would raise confidence to 0.961 🟢 |

### The Option B version

Only square footage clears a materiality threshold of, say, 0.005, so Option B spends **two calls**: one at 2,537 sq ft (18% larger) and one at 1,892 sq ft (12% smaller). Suppose Verisk returns \$423,500 and \$332,000:

| | Option A (table) | Option B (calls) |
|---|:-:|:-:|
| Impact if 18% larger | +15.1% | +13.8% |
| Impact if 12% smaller | −10.3% | −10.8% |
| Confidence $C$ | 0.893 🟡 | 0.896 🟡 |

The exact calls show the familiar economies of scale: a bigger home adds a little less than the table assumed, and a smaller home loses a little more. The tier doesn't change, which is typical. Option B matters most when a home is unusual for its stratum.

### What if the \$/sqft had looked off?

| If MERC per sq ft were… | $z$ | Result |
|---|:-:|---|
| \$173 (actual) | −0.6 | No change: 🟡 Medium |
| \$150 | −2.1 | Drop one tier: 🔴 Low |
| \$130 | −3.5 | Force 🔴 Low |

