## 11. Proving it works

> **In one sentence:** compare predicted confidence with what actually happens on a random audit sample, and fix gaps by adjusting the inputs, never by fitting a model on MERC.

### 11.1 Where the truth comes from

| Source | Quality | Notes |
|---|:-:|---|
| **Audit arm** | Good | A random 2–5% of quotes where every material attribute is asked. Ideally the prefill value isn't shown, since people anchor on what they see. |
| **Post-bind inspections** | Best | Where available |
| **Normal member corrections** | Biased | We only see corrections on the attributes we asked about |

> ⚠️ **Why the audit arm matters:** in the normal flow we never ask about high-confidence attributes, so we never learn when they were wrong, and those are exactly the errors this score predicts. The audit arm is the only unbiased view.

### 11.2 What we can check

For each audit quote we know both the attributes the normal flow *would* have used and the fully confirmed ones. One extra Verisk call per audit quote gives the **realized leftover error**:

$$
D_{\text{realized}} \;=\; \ln \text{MERC}_{\text{fully confirmed}} \;-\; \ln \text{MERC}_{\text{normal flow}}
$$

Engine error is different. It's only observable in rare total losses, so that part of the score rests on Verisk's stated range.

| Check | What good looks like |
|---|---|
| **Calibration** | Among quotes with attribute-only confidence near 0.80, about 80% have $D_{\text{realized}}$ within tolerance |
| **Tier separation** | Realized errors grow clearly from High to Medium to Low |
| **Neighborhood override** | Flagged quotes show larger realized errors than unflagged quotes in the same base tier |
| **Beats simple baselines** | Outperforms "product of the $p_k$" and "gap between the prefill and MLE MERCs" |

### 11.3 Fixing miscalibration

Adjust the inputs, never a MERC model:

| Symptom | Likely fix |
|---|---|
| Errors are bigger than predicted across the board | Correction profiles are too narrow, or attribute confidences are too high |
| Errors pile up on the same homes | Add correlation between attribute errors ([section 14](#14-known-limitations)) |
| One attribute keeps surprising us | Revisit its sensitivity or its screening threshold |
| Too many or too few quotes land in High | Revisit the tolerances and tier cutoffs |

### 11.4 Monitoring

Review the tier mix, audit results and override rate every quarter, and again after every Verisk data update.

